{:edb/db-before-t 2497 :edb/db-after-t 2498 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535810 50 #inst "2026-10-09T07:47:15.004Z" 13194139535810 true] [17592186179737 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367610 13194139535810 true] [17592186179737 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367617 13194139535810 true] [17592186179737 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367621 13194139535810 true] [17592186179738 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367603 13194139535810 true] [17592186179738 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367624 13194139535810 true] [17592186367603 1017 #edb/ref 17592186367604 13194139535810 true] [17592186367603 1089 "q-119647" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367603 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535810 true] [17592186367603 1100 "At a geological site, a sample of a lunar rock is found with $84%$ of its original rubidium-87 content. Given that the half-life of rubidium-87 is $49.72$ billion years, approximately how old is the sample?" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367603 1113 #uuid "112894ef-795f-5e88-92b6-8cecf3a4c053" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367603 1145 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=49.72$ billion years, we have\n\n$$\n49.72=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{49.72}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.84{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.84{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.84{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ 0.84\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ 0.84 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{49.72} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{49.72\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈12.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the sample is approximately $12.5$ billion years old." 13194139535810 true] [17592186367604 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367605 13194139535810 true] [17592186367604 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367606 13194139535810 true] [17592186367604 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367607 13194139535810 true] [17592186367604 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367608 13194139535810 true] [17592186367604 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367609 13194139535810 true] [17592186367604 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535810 true] [17592186367604 1041 "selection" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367604 1104 #edb/ref 17592186367607 13194139535810 true] [17592186367604 1110 #uuid "52e3ecb4-a435-50af-bcae-f491b642011c" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367605 1034 "$3.125$ billion years" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367605 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535810 true] [17592186367605 1111 #uuid "ef74b3a6-ea54-5296-b5de-f662f60f6903" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367606 1034 "$6.25$ billion years" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367606 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535810 true] [17592186367606 1111 #uuid "18de7856-6904-5cfa-874d-cee4bb529a20" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367607 1034 "$12.5$ billion years" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367607 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535810 true] [17592186367607 1111 #uuid "9c6487d9-4ace-5001-a38b-eabb716a3bff" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367608 1034 "$50$ billion years" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367608 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535810 true] [17592186367608 1111 #uuid "04df3361-f2dd-5d0e-b922-98fe3040f169" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367609 1034 "$25$ billion years" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367609 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535810 true] [17592186367609 1111 #uuid "1806677e-9da0-5bca-84b4-900ac3902ea8" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367610 1017 #edb/ref 17592186367611 13194139535810 true] [17592186367610 1089 "q-120002" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367610 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535810 true] [17592186367610 1100 "Samarium-147 has a half-life of $106.1$ billion years. A sample of samarium-147 has an initial mass of $300\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Rounded to the nearest integer, what is the mass of the sample after $80$ billion years?" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367610 1113 #uuid "eba20180-55df-5c61-831d-b8f257c1e722" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367610 1145 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=106.1$ billion years, we have\n\n$$\n106.1=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{106.1}.\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=300\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=300{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/106.1}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nM(80)=300{e}^{-80\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/106.1}≈178\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that there will be $178\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ of the sample left." 13194139535810 true] [17592186367611 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367612 13194139535810 true] [17592186367611 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367613 13194139535810 true] [17592186367611 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367614 13194139535810 true] [17592186367611 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367615 13194139535810 true] [17592186367611 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367616 13194139535810 true] [17592186367611 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535810 true] [17592186367611 1041 "selection" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367611 1104 #edb/ref 17592186367616 13194139535810 true] [17592186367611 1110 #uuid "32273387-4de6-5518-9523-f7f689666c55" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367612 1034 "168\\,\\mu \\text{g}" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367612 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535810 true] [17592186367612 1111 #uuid "f2d84dfd-c9e6-5003-ac7c-e9ea0ec3fd1d" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367613 1034 "257\\,\\mu \\text{g}" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367613 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535810 true] [17592186367613 1111 #uuid "01e53487-1b14-5372-8e4f-20071bc6c649" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367614 1034 "185\\,\\mu \\text{g}" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367614 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535810 true] [17592186367614 1111 #uuid "ee0b9056-1f22-5cc1-945f-a81d63b7230e" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367615 1034 "154\\,\\mu \\text{g}" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367615 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535810 true] [17592186367615 1111 #uuid "eb9a9e04-70d2-51fc-abd2-ad17a26de2ec" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367616 1034 "178\\,\\mu \\text{g}" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367616 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535810 true] [17592186367616 1111 #uuid "57904937-370c-5d16-b386-208372b8cdd9" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367617 1017 #edb/ref 17592186367618 13194139535810 true] [17592186367617 1089 "q-353520" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367617 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535810 true] [17592186367617 1100 "The half-life of cobalt-60 is $5.27$ years. How long does it take for a $8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ sample of cobalt-60 to reduce to $0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{yr}$" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367617 1113 #uuid "df0d388b-d8e7-5b69-9a20-a4a8e6fb4a42" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367617 1145 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=5.27$ years, we have\n\n$$\n5.27=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.27}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=8.0{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.5 & =8.0{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.5 & =8.0{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27} \\\\ \\frac{0.5}{8.0} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27} \\\\ \\frac{1}{16} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{16}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡16 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.27} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡16 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.27} \\\\ t & =\\frac{5.27\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡16}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{5.27\\cdot 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =21.08\\,\\text{yr}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}}{8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $4$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ to $0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=4\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=4\\cdot 5.27=21.08\\,\\text{yr}.\n$$" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367618 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367619 13194139535810 true] [17592186367618 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367620 13194139535810 true] [17592186367618 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535810 true] [17592186367618 1041 "field-1" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367618 1104 #edb/ref 17592186367619 13194139535810 true] [17592186367618 1110 #uuid "0f57081c-0f21-526d-ab87-a528d56fa49e" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367619 1034 "21.08" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367619 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535810 true] [17592186367619 1111 #uuid "677520ea-2209-5c86-9d83-7003a2404f56" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367620 1034 "10.54" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367620 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535810 true] [17592186367620 1111 #uuid "662c228f-45b6-59b0-b6ae-d0b8458abef0" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367621 1017 #edb/ref 17592186367622 13194139535810 true] [17592186367621 1089 "q-353523" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367621 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535810 true] [17592186367621 1100 "The half-life of fluorine-18 is $109.7$ minutes. How long does it take for a $6.4\\,\\text{ng}$ sample of fluorine-18 to reduce to $0.2\\,\\text{ng}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{min}$" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367621 1113 #uuid "f0f317b5-d8f7-537b-9329-3365ba5a0f20" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367621 1145 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=109.7$ minutes, we have\n\n$$\n109.7=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{109.7}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=6.4\\,\\text{ng},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=6.4{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.2\\,\\text{ng}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.2 & =6.4{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.2 & =6.4{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7} \\\\ \\frac{0.2}{6.4} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{32} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{32}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡32 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{109.7} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡32 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{109.7} \\\\ t & =\\frac{109.7\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡32}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{109.7\\cdot 5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =548.5\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.2\\,\\text{ng}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{0.2\\,\\text{ng}}{6.4\\,\\text{ng}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $5$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $6.4\\,\\text{ng}$ to $0.2\\,\\text{ng}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=5\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=5\\cdot 109.7=548.5\\,\\text{min}.\n$$" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367622 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367623 13194139535810 true] [17592186367622 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535810 true] [17592186367622 1041 "field-1" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367622 1104 #edb/ref 17592186367623 13194139535810 true] [17592186367622 1110 #uuid "e9c6f2b4-fb25-536b-9947-ace029ba1cc1" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367623 1034 "548.5" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367623 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535810 true] [17592186367623 1111 #uuid "67457bd7-88fc-5451-9373-e093db91fd03" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367624 1017 #edb/ref 17592186367625 13194139535810 true] [17592186367624 1089 "q-355651" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367624 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535810 true] [17592186367624 1100 "At a deep-sea sediment site, a sample is found with $30%$ of its original beryllium-10 content. Given that the half-life of beryllium-10 is $1.39$ million years, how old is the sample? Round your answer to **one decimal place**.\n\nThe sample is approximately\n{{field-1}}\nmillion years old." 13194139535810 true] [17592186367624 1113 #uuid "607cd4b5-ba88-56bb-b14d-3e00fe0f1f1a" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367624 1145 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=1.39$ million years, we have\n\n$$\n1.39=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{1.39}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.30{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.30{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.30{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39} \\\\ 0.30\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39} \\\\ 0.30 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.30) & =ln\\,({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.30) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{1.39} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1.39\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.30)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈2.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the sample is approximately $2.4$ million years old." 13194139535810 true] [17592186367625 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367626 13194139535810 true] [17592186367625 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535810 true] [17592186367625 1041 "field-1" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367625 1104 #edb/ref 17592186367626 13194139535810 true] [17592186367625 1110 #uuid "22b693eb-17a4-55c0-9906-f969af7c9551" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367626 1034 "2.4" 13194139535810 true] [17592186367626 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535810 true] [17592186367626 1111 #uuid "2cbeaaa0-f83b-5e1f-81b5-53530e4d01ee" 13194139535810 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-119647" 17592186367603 "q-119647/selection" 17592186367604 "q-119647/selection/2d2d1e32cbc95f79e789f5fe5a26a710b51d015365ef2c0d6f24389583105fa7" 17592186367605 "q-119647/selection/2f8ae7055dd2918f9f136e391a6bf6cb56ed4b10e7a8d45bf02d16553a78b61e" 17592186367606 "q-119647/selection/55a65e6aa2a871ce970b7cb50a6e5a40ece791f29a126b778b888842fc9a5795" 17592186367607 "q-119647/selection/5b97079dcdbe655ecfdc9590da541c256e7ba8f467a2f82f79bd2f6b0e61e5a3" 17592186367608 "q-119647/selection/bf740a08f91a7ae2d0a2c4a71a9bd3bc09483739a2f9fffc7550fd4199c527db" 17592186367609 "q-120002" 17592186367610 "q-120002/selection" 17592186367611 "q-120002/selection/0e9e4d9b183b9bf90110c6abeb12b7b174c7da3206dfc265a0bb0210174725c6" 17592186367612 "q-120002/selection/19a77a7c62c860ae14ef0645b1e5d2f33f4b6769456f1b3a466c293029ced86e" 17592186367613 "q-120002/selection/c355b15b1a23c12fe4141e6c53356b2779ace8ec93703cf74a93da1937d1766a" 17592186367614 "q-120002/selection/cb190f66c68857c315dbc9c5ad3da9e3ae3fade16c90445e8324435fcac83561" 17592186367615 "q-120002/selection/ce483bf90cc56f42c7ad168bb2903d6d9cdce52f55627ce458904ad333721ed6" 17592186367616 "q-353520" 17592186367617 "q-353520/field-1" 17592186367618 "q-353520/field-1/727a4fb7f2c1cbd59f3c8a0afcc719a1ca6d